meta {
  name: Sign up (email and password)
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/sign-up/email
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Origin: {{webOrigin}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "lucamandelli@example.com",
    "password": "luca-de-obra-123",
    "name": "Luca CTO"
  }
}

docs {
  Creates the account and returns the session in the same response — there is
  no email verification step, and there will be none until a transactional
  email provider exists (issue #11). It is the only way an account is created
  today; in the finished product accounts come from invitations, and this
  self-signup leaves in epic #11.

  The body returns the created user and sets the session cookie, so you can go
  straight to "Current session" without signing in.

  **The same request creates the whole tenant.** Along with the user come that
  person's organization, a membership with default role `admin`, and **three
  free licenses**. No second client call does this: if the network dropped
  between the two, an orphan user would be left behind — and the server is the
  one that decides who is admin of what.

  No route exposes those rows yet, so check them in the database. The quickest
  way is `bun run --filter @bluprint/api db:studio`, which opens the tables in
  the browser. By query:

      docker compose exec postgres psql -U bluprint -d bluprint

      select o.name, m.role, count(l.id) as licenses
      from "user" u
      join member m on m.user_id = u.id
      join organization o on o.id = m.organization_id
      left join license l on l.organization_id = o.id
      where u.email = 'lucamandelli@example.com'
      group by o.name, m.role;

  Expect one row, `role = admin` and `licenses = 3`. Signing in again through
  the sign-in route does **not** seed again — repeating the query after signing
  in still returns 3.

  **Responses you will see**

  | Status | When |
  | --- | --- |
  | 200 | Account created, session and tenant returned |
  | 400 | Password shorter than 8 characters — body `PASSWORD_TOO_SHORT` |
  | 422 | Email already registered — body `USER_ALREADY_EXISTS_USE_ANOTHER_EMAIL` |
  | 403 | `ALLOW_SELF_SIGNUP` off on the server — body `SELF_SIGNUP_DISABLED` |
  | 403 | `Origin` missing or `null` — body `MISSING_OR_NULL_ORIGIN` |
  | 429 | More than 5 sign-ups in the same minute |

  **The two 403s are unrelated.** One is the self-signup flag, the other is
  `trustedOrigins`. Same status, different causes — read the body's `code`
  before touching the `Origin` header, or lose half an hour.

  The flag is read at boot, and `nest start --watch` watches `apps/api/src`, not
  the root `.env`: changing `ALLOW_SELF_SIGNUP` requires restarting the API.

  `isPlatformAdmin` is ignored if you send it in the body. The field is
  `input: false` on purpose: only the server decides who is platform admin, and
  that boundary is what keeps project content away from anyone who is not a
  member. Try sending `"isPlatformAdmin": true` and check that the response
  comes back `false`.

  **No `assert`, on purpose.** The request is not idempotent — the body's email
  has to be changed by hand on every run, and a `res.status: eq 200` would turn
  red on the second, which trains people to ignore red asserts. The
  `*.int-spec.ts` files are what prove the contract.
}
